WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:03.490 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  2017 is far from over and yet there have  been an astounding number of games  already released this year you likely  heard about Resident Evil 7 and Mass  Effect Andromeda but numerous other  games have launched in 2017 that you may  have missed and are still well worth  checking out we've rounded up six such  games for Xbox one but be sure to hit  the subscribe button below to get our  recommendations on other platforms in  the coming days  [Music]  one game that may have gone unnoticed  but is worth a look is thimble we'd park  a point-and-click adventure by famed ex  LucasArts duo Ron Gilbert and Gary  Winnick tumbleweed park deals with a  murder in the small town of thimble weed  park that two FBI agents have to  investigate it rides a thin line between  nostalgia and reinvention balancing its  comically written nods to the genre with  some modern design choices well it  sometimes sticks too closely to the  point click genres long-held mechanics  then what we park is great for  experience adventure game fans eager to  experience a whimsical narrative and  solve some puzzles  Forza horizon 3 was already arguably the  best racing game of the generation and  it's only gotten better through DLC  their first expansion took players to a  wintery landscape but this year's  hotwheels DLC is the stuff of childhood  fantasies it allows you to get behind  the wheel of classic Hot Wheels cars and  drive them around wild course is  comprised of loops and huge jumps better  yet the courses are constructed from the  signature orange Hot Wheels tracks these  weave around some gorgeous island  settings and feature new boost pads that  provide a sense of speed unlike anything  else in port so it's downright  exhilarating when you leap across a  chasm or go zooming past a giant  animatronic dinosaur for the first time  ukulele was originally pitched as a  spiritual successor to banjo kazooie and  that certainly wasn't an exaggeration  like the classic Nintendo 64 platformer  ukulele stars in a penny miss duo in  this case a lizard named Yuka and a bat  named Leigh Leigh and has players used  their abilities to explore colorful  worlds and pick apart all manner of  collectibles ukulele channel's the  spirit of banjo kazooie so well that it  genuinely feels like a long-lost antenna  64 platformer for better and sometimes  for worse but even during its moments of  frustration ukulele remains one of the  most charming releases of the year  thanks to its tongue-in-cheek writing  beautiful art style and whimsical  soundtrack while it may not top at  banjos first two adventures it certainly  sets along rares antenna 64 output  nicely anyone who grew up with nineties  platformers and has a fondness for rare  games in particular will find a lot to  love about ukulele  roguelikes have become wildly popular in  recent years but few have taken as  interesting of an approach to the genre  as crypt of the necrodancer it tasks  players but navigating a dungeon to the  beat of the music instead of just moving  are attacking freely you and your  enemies actions are tied directly to the  excellent soundtrack further  complicating things is the need to  always be doing something if you skip a  beat without acting your combo resets  which means you'll earn less gold and  potentially do less damage depending on  what items you've acquired especially as  the beat of the music increases there's  real tension and excitement to every new  room you enter and you have to  constantly consider what you want to do  next and how enemies might respond it's  an experience that's quite unlike  anything else and it's one that you'll  certainly want to try  [Music]  the sexy Brutale is a quirky little  puzzle game co-developed by tequila  works the studio behind the beautiful  adventure game rhyme it's essentially  Groundhog Day the game you play through  the same day over and over but with each  run-through you learn more about the  creepy mansion you find yourself in  after seeing one character shoot another  you might go and find a gun and prevent  the bloody murder by replacing real  bullets with blanks a number of these  murders are interconnected solving one  puzzle might prevent one murder but that  could change another branch of time  elsewhere in the house but discovering  and tinkering with different timelines  is where the fun lies we've played it  over and over again Groundhog Day indeed  being a roguelike style shooter enter  the gungeon naturally draws comparisons  to games like The Binding of Isaac and  nuclear throne and while that helps you  understand what it's like to play enter  the gungeon manages to rise above being  a pale imitator it feels fantastic with  a dodge roll ability that allows you to  satisfyingly evade damage with a  well-timed use there are ridiculous  weapons such as the one that fires bees  or a gun that shoots guns which  themselves fire bullets the procedurally  generated environments are well crafted  and helped keep each run feeling fresh  as do the wide variety of items and  secrets that you uncover along the way  and co-op support makes for an  especially fun and chaotic experience  the entire game is also overflowing with  personality and color making for an  experience that is as fun to look at as  it is to play this is just a small  selection of the great Xbox one games  that came out this year which you might  have missed and of course there are  plenty more so be sure to subscribe to  GameSpot for all of our Xbox one  coverage and stay tuned in the coming  days for future videos where we take a  look at hidden gems on PlayStation 4  Nintendo switch and PC  [Music]

